    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d76">

                <pb facs="0173" n="71" xml:id="pg71"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/426: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-06-22T20:00:00+03:00">June 22, 1935—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 22—5:16 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>250. The British Ambassador who has just returned from London told me today that
                    he had informed Litvinov<anchor n="24" xml:id="d76-f1"/> officially yesterday
                    that the British Government intended to call a general naval conference and that
                    an invitation to the Soviet Government to participate in such a conference
                    positively would be extended.</p>
                <p>The British Ambassador stated that he had had many conversations with
                        Vansittart<anchor n="25" xml:id="d76-f2"/> and members of the British
                    Cabinet with regard to the situation in the Far East and that he was certain
                    that Great Britain would not intervene in any way to prevent the Japanese from
                    extending their control over the whole of North China.</p>
                <p>I asked him if British interests in North China were to be sacrificed completely,
                    and he replied, “Nothing will stop Japan except war and we have no more
                    intention of going to war with Japan than you have”.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d76-f1" n="24" xml:id="d76-f1-content">
                <p>Maxim M. Litvinov, Soviet Commissar for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d76-f2" n="25" xml:id="d76-f2-content">
                <p>Sir Robert Gilbert Vansittart, British Permanent Under Secretary of State for
                    Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
